we've been hearing about Apple beginning  to switch their max over to their very  own ARM based chips for a couple of  years now but we're finally starting to  hear some specific details on how and  when Apple will begin this process so in  this video we'll discuss some of those  rumors and figure out whether it's worth  waiting for apples are based Mac's or  not about a month and a half ago  Bloomberg reported that Apple will  release its first Mac powered by an ARM  processor in 2021  apparently it'll be based on the a14  chip which were expecting to see in the  2021 iPhones which will be built using a  brand new 5 nanometer process which is  going to be extremely efficient they  mentioned that this chip will be a  12-course CPU with eight  high-performance cores and for  energy-efficient course now you might be  thinking that this means it'll be a very  powerful processor meant to go into a  pro machine like the MacBook Pro but  it'll most likely be meant for lighter  workloads more on that in just a minute  Bloomberg also mentioned that Apple has  been working and testing a prototype  chip that's based on the a 12 X chip  which came in the 2018 iPad pro and the  testing was successful enough that they  decided to begin the transition as early  as 2020 and one last piece of info is  that switching to arm is expected to  save Apple about 40 to 60 percent on  their processor costs compared to the  current chips from Intel that's actually  a huge amount but don't expect these  rumored armed max to get significantly  cheaper since consumers will swarm to  buy these Macs  because of all of the hype even at  current prices now the most important  thing for us to figure out is which of  these Mac's will be the first to come  with ARM processors one thing's for sure  is that we should not expect the ARM  chip to come to the machines that are  used for professional work like the  MacBook Pro the Mac Mini or the iMac  there will most likely be a period of  around 1 or 2 years where not everything  will work properly on the first arm Mac  Apple has to give developers some time  to rewrite their apps  to support these new chips insist those  Macs that I mentioned are used by  professionals to get important work done  Apple will not risk switching those  Mac's over until Mac OS runs flawlessly  with these new chips and that could take  at least two years if not four or five  so if you're in the market for a  high-powered MacBook Pro for  professional work and you're waiting on  the arm Macs to come don't hold your  breath  Apple is much more likely to put their  new ARM chip into an entry-level Mac  like the MacBook Air or they could even  revive the 12-inch MacBook the reason  they would do this is because those Mac  books were never meant for any type of  professional work  they were meant for web browsing using  basic apps watching YouTube or movies in  doing work that requires a lot of typing  and we're already able to do these tasks  on the iPad pro with the magic keyboard  case which is pretty awesome if you guys  haven't seen that review you guys can  check it out after this video so for  those users they won't really care that  a lot of third-party apps might not be  compatible on day one because they don't  use those apps like MacBook Pro users do  all that would really hear about is  battery life and having enough power to  keep it feeling snappy and that's  exactly what an ARM chip will give you  incredible battery life and great  performance without getting very hot and  without a lot of fan noise which is  ironically the situation with the 20/20  MacBook Air which gets really hot and  really loud especially the i5 and i7  models we all wondered why Apple didn't  connect the fan to the heatsink on the  MacBook Air well maybe it's because  they're planning to put an ARM chip in  it next year where it will no longer  need a fan at all and it won't get  nearly as hot as the current air  consumers looking for a basic laptop are  gonna love it and there's a chance that  Apple will allow us games to run on that  arm MacBook and maybe that's why we've  been seeing rumors of Apple coming out  with their very own gaming controller  that would totally make sense especially  since we already have the Apple arcade  for Mac OS which the controller will def  only be used for so there's really a lot  to speculate about on this upcoming arm  MacBook but thankfully there's a really  good chance that Apple will mention it  at this year's WWDC on June 2nd so we  can all look forward to that but the  most important thing that you need to  realize is that if you're looking for a  high powered Mac for a creative or  professional work it doesn't really make  sense to wait and you should consider  buying one of the current Mac's that are  on sale using the links in the  description below so if this video helps  you out go ahead and click the circle  above to subscribe for our WWDC coverage  coming later this month and definitely  be sure to check below this video for  our new line of t-shirts thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]
 

